Evaluating lox expressions we execute the syntax. Different interpreters do this step differently eg: convert to machine code, bytecode etc. What we will be doing is execute the python code in which our interpreter is written in to produce values. Python provides garbabe collection and is dynamically typed similar to how Lox is designed. Python also provides isinstance builtin function we can use to selectively execute the operations based on the operands, eg: concatanating two strings when + operator is used between two operands.
We will use the following mapping between python and Lox.
Lox                         Python
Any Lox value               object
nil                         None
Boolean                     bool
number                      float
string                      str
Python is not a statically typed language. As such the mapping will not be enforced by the language but we will use the typing functionality to encode our mapping.
In the book a design pattern - Visitor - is used to implement the logic of each types executing the operator. In Python we have match statement which allows us to implement the functionality of selectively executing block of code based on the type in a direct manner. We will be using that to implement the operations. This becomes a pretty significant evaluate method but is fairly readable and all the code lives in one function.
Evaluating literals is pretty straight forward. We simply pull out the value since it was already eagerly created in the parser when creating the nodes of the syntax tree.
Evaluating grouping requires evaluating inside the parenthesis. For this we return a recursive call to the sub-expression inside the parenthesis.
case Grouping(expr):
        return self.evaluate(expr)

Evaluating unary operator we evaluate the expression first (similar to grouping) and then apply the unary operator. For minus we make sure that the expression evaluates the number type and for ! we ensure the expression evaluates to a boolean type. In discussing boolean the concept of what evaluate to true and false needs to be clarified. Different languages have different ideas on what evaluates to true and false. In Lox we will only consider false and nil as fasly - meaning evaluates to false - and everything else will evaluate to true.
Evaluating binary operators multiply, divide and minus operators are straight forward. We evaluate the left and right operands; make sure they are of type number and return the value of resulting expression. For plus operator we will be supporting concatenation of string as well. This requires selectively checking both operands and then applying the appropriate operation. Since Python behaves the same way it is straight forward to implement it.
case TokenType.PLUS:
        if isinstance(left_value, str) and isinstance(right_value, str):
                return left_value + right_value
        if isinstance(left_value, float) and isinstance(
                right_value, float
        ):
                return left_value + right_value
Evaluating comparison operators &lt;, &lt;=, &gt; and &gt;= we check that the operands are of type number and evaluate the expression. For = and != we do not support any implicit conversions. Consequently 3 and "3" do not evaluate as equal. We implement this by ensuring that both operands are of the same types before checking the equality. If the type check fails the equality check immediately fails.
Runtime Error: Errors in parsing and scanning are caught by the Scanner and Parser class. There is another class of errors we need to consider which are runtime errors. There are errors that are not caught by the scanner and parser. For example it does not make sense to divide one string with another "undefined" / 3;. If we did not provide Lox specific error handling for operations like this the user would see error trace from Python which is not user friendly. We would like to provide Lox specific error handling. When the user is executing expressions using REPL we also would like to allow the user to continue in the REPL without exiting if one of the executions turns out to contain error.
We do this by creating Lox specific error handling class LoxRuntimeError which inherits from python builtin RuntimeError exception.
@staticmethod
def _require_number(operator: Token, value: object) -&gt; float:
        """Confirm that value is a float and return it."""
        if type(value) is float:
                return value
        raise LoxRuntimeError(operator, "Operand must be a number.")
Should a runtime error be detected we use the try/except Python functionality to gracefully inform the user that a runtime error has occurred.
def interpret(self, expression: Expr) -&gt; None:
        """Evaluate the expression and print the value."""
        try:
                value: object = self.evaluate(expression)
                print(self._stringify(value))
        except LoxRuntimeError as error:
                ErrorReporter.runtime_error(error)
The runtime_error function consumes the exception and prints a user friendly message specific to the error token.
The interpret method evaluates the expression and prints the result to standard output. The _stringify method contains string formatting logic specific Lox that differes from Python.
def _stringify(self, obj: object) -&gt; str:
        """Convert a Lox value to its display representation."""
        if obj is None:
                return "nil"
        if isinstance(obj, float):
                text = str(obj)
                if text.endswith(".0"):
                        return text[:-2]
        if isinstance(obj, bool):
                # Lox booleans are lowercase.
                text = str(obj)
                return text.lower()
        return str(obj)
That is all there is to it.

In this post I am going to do a shallow walkthrough of the Parser class which implements the parsing logic for the interpreter. Parser consumes the list of tokens created by the Scanner class and produces a syntax tree or Abstract Syntax Tree based on the grammar of the language.
In this section we are only going to talk about expressions (not statements). Simple way to think of expression is that it evaluates to a value. statements do things like print a string. The rules of the grammar dictate valid expressions. Our Parser generates error messages for illformed expressions.
Here is an example of a simple grammar that can be used to create expressions.
expression     → literal | unary | binary | grouping ;
literal        → NUMBER | STRING | "true" | "false" | "nil" ;
grouping       → "(" expression ")" ;
unary          → ( "-" | "!" ) expression ;
binary         → expression operator expression ;
operator       → "==" | "!=" | "&lt;" | "&lt;=" | "&gt;" | "&gt;=" | "+"  | "-"  | "*" | "/" ;
Though simple to create expressions the issue we run into is ambiguity. We can use the same grammar to produce both (6 / 3) - 1 or 6 / (3 - 1). This is not good for our parser because based on the rules it can’t identify which one is the correct one. We fix this by using operator precedence and associativity which makes the grammar complex but removes the ambiguity issue.
Here is the new grammar:
expression     → equality ;
equality       → comparison ( ( "!=" | "==" ) comparison )* ;
comparison     → term ( ( "&gt;" | "&gt;=" | "&lt;" | "&lt;=" ) term )* ;
term           → factor ( ( "-" | "+" ) factor )* ;
factor         → unary ( ( "/" | "*" ) unary )* ;
unary          → ( "!" | "-" ) unary
               | primary ;
primary        → NUMBER | STRING | "true" | "false" | "nil"
               | "(" expression ")" ;
The operator precedence increases as we go down the list. So unary has higher precedence than comparison. This also makes intuitive sense. In -1 == 2 we expect -1 to take higher precedence than ==. The grammar rules are implemented as functions where some are recursive in themselves (like unary) or through other functions (like expression which can fall through to primary). The grammar rules with * are the binary operators and encode associativity.
Here we take a look at one of the rules which is implemented as a function. Recursive Descent parsing is the technique used to chain the function calls and implement the parser.
def _comparison(self) -&gt; Expr:
        """Parse comparison operators (&lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=) left-associatively."""
        expr: Expr = self._term()
        expr = self._binary_helper(
                expr,
                self._term,
                TokenType.LESS,
                TokenType.LESS_EQUAL,
                TokenType.GREATER,
                TokenType.GREATER_EQUAL,
        )
        return expr 

def _binary_helper(
        self, expr: Expr, parse_operand: Callable[[], Expr], *types: TokenType
) -&gt; Expr:
        """Parse a left-associative chain of binary operators using parse_operand for each operand."""
        while self._match(*types):
                operator: Token = self._previous()
                right: Expr = parse_operand()
                expr = Binary(expr, operator, right)
        return expr
As specified by the rule, first thing to do is evaluate the higher precedence token sequence (if any) which is done in self._term() function call. After that we make use of the _binary_helper method to create a binary expression accounting for associativity in the case of sequence of operators. We do this with the while loop.
The token streams are consumed by the functions and appropriate nodes in the syntax tree are created.
But ofcourse we have to talk about what happens when something goes wrong in the event that the token sequence are illformed. In that case we have error reporting functions that provide high level clue on where in the file the potential error exists and possibly clues on which token.
And pretty much covers at a high level the operations of the Parser class. Main objectives are to consume the token stream and create a Abstract Syntax Tree and provide decent error reporting to help the user correct the code.
